<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

  Product overlay for SettingsProvider's Settings.Global defaults. Only the three
  values below are overridden; everything else keeps the AOSP default, because a
  DEVICE_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS resource file is merged per resource name rather than
  swapped in whole.

  These defaults apply to a FRESH userdata only. SettingsProvider seeds
  Settings.Global once, on the first boot of an empty /data. On a board whose
  /data is already provisioned, set the same values live instead:

      settings put global bluetooth_on 0        (or: svc bluetooth disable)
      settings put global window_animation_scale 0
      settings put global transition_animation_scale 0
      settings put global animator_duration_scale 0    (no resource default
                                                        exists; runtime only)

  def_bluetooth_on=false  (DOES NOT TAKE EFFECT ON AAOS; kept for the record)
    Measured on a fresh userdata, 2026-08-18 on the RPi5 P4 image: the two animation
    scales below came up at 0.0 as intended, but bluetooth_on was still 1. AAOS ships
    CarSettingsProviderConfigRRO (packages/services/Car/car_product/rro/
    overlay-config/SettingsProviderRRO/res/values/defaults.xml line 24) which sets
    def_bluetooth_on=true, and a runtime resource overlay outranks a build-time
    DEVICE_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS resource. Beating it would mean shipping our own RRO with
    a higher priority; until someone does, turn Bluetooth off at run time:
      svc bluetooth disable        (persists in /data)
    The value below is left in place because it is correct for a non-automotive
    product and because removing it would hide why the runtime step is needed.

    There is no Bluetooth transport in this guest. AAOS brings the adapter up
    anyway, the HAL never becomes ready, and com.android.bluetooth crash loops:
    measured on hardware 2026-08-18 it restarted about every 44 s, each time
    running crash_dump64 and writing a tombstone to /data, which reaches the SD
    through DomD's device model, so the cost lands on the whole cockpit. The
    console shows the framework giving up on it:
      GceEventReporter: VIRTUAL_DEVICE_BOOT_FAILED: Dependencies not ready
                        after 10 checks: Bluetooth
    Leaving the adapter off stops the loop. The packages stay installed, so
    turning Bluetooth on by hand still works if a transport is ever added.

  def_window_animation_scale / def_window_transition_scale = 0%
    Window and transition animations are pure compositor work on a platform whose
    GPU is not the bottleneck: with three Settings/Home transitions driven in a
    loop, V3D render sat at about 24% while DomD's device model burned 83% of a
    core translating and copying frames. Turning the two scales off removes that
    work from every screen change. Measured together with loglevel=4 and
    Bluetooth off: DomD went from 120.4% to 89.6% of a core over the same
    workload. Set them to 50% instead of 0% if the demo wants the motion back at
    half the cost.

  Note for editors: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen, which is why
  this block uses none.
-->
<resources>
    <bool name="def_bluetooth_on">false</bool>
    <fraction name="def_window_animation_scale">0%</fraction>
    <fraction name="def_window_transition_scale">0%</fraction>
</resources>
